<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Front for the Restoration of Unity and Democracy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1991">1991</a>–present</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as FRUD</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Front for the Restoration of Unity and Democracy (FRUD) is a political movement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djibouti">Djibouti</a>, formed in 1991. It launched an armed rebellion that year against the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-gouled-aptidon">Hassan Gouled Aptidon</a>, protesting at the dominance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/issa-clan">Issa Somali</a>. A peace accord in 1994 brought its main wing into government, and a radical faction fought on until agreements of 2000 and 2001.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/front-for-the-restoration-of-unity-and-democracy.jpg" alt="Front for the Restoration of Unity and Democracy" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Germenfer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Front_for_the_Restoration_of_Unity_and_Democracy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>FRUD came into being in 1991 through the merger of three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> groups: Action for the Revision of Order in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djibouti">Djibouti</a>, the Front for the Restoration of Right and Equality, and the Djibouti Patriotic Resistance Front. Late in 1991 it took up arms against the Issa-dominated government, and nearly 1,000 people died in the conflict up to 1994. The movement speaks for the interests of the Afar of Djibouti, but it has also drawn supporters living outside the country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1994 the movement split in two. A moderate wing under Ali Mohamed Daoud signed a peace agreement with the government at Aba'a on 26 December 1994. A second wing under Ahmed Dini Ahmed rejected the accord. Its supporters had already held a six-day congress in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djibouti">Djibouti</a> in late September, announcing on 30 September that the delegates were resolved to pursue armed struggle until their political goals were met, and electing Dini to head their executive committee.</p><p>The radicals, known as FRUD-C (Combatant) or the Ahmed Dini faction, fought on with arms and probably logistical help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, trying to cut <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djibouti">Djibouti</a>'s supply routes to landlocked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> during the Eritrean–Ethiopian War. Attacks under the FRUD-C name did not cease entirely: a policeman was killed in January 2021, and on 7 October 2022 an assault on the barracks at Garabtisan killed seven soldiers. The moderate faction joined the government in condemning it as unjustifiable in a time of political pluralism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Under the 1994 agreement the moderate faction received two ministries on 8 June 1995: Daoud became Minister of Health and Social Affairs and the secretary-general Ougoure Kifle Ahmed Minister of Agriculture and Water Resources. The party was legalised in March 1996. At its first congress, on 15–16 April 1997, it elected a 153-member National Council and a 21-member Executive Committee that included Issa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadabuursi">Gadabursi</a>, Arab and Isaaq members, among them the Issa Djama Djellai as First Vice-President.</p><p>FRUD then allied with the ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-rally-for-progress">People's Rally for Progress</a>. Their joint list won all 65 seats of the National Assembly in December 1997, and in April 1999 the party backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-omar-guelleh">Ismail Omar Guelleh</a>, who took 74.02% of the presidential vote. In the elections of 2003 and 2008 it stood within the Union for the Presidential Majority, while the radical faction ran with the opposition in 2003. Daoud was re-elected party president in 2002 and again in 2007.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Front for the Restoration of Unity and Democracy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Afar grievances led to the <b>FRUD</b> rebellion of 1991 to 1994, ended by power-sharing agreements.</blockquote><a href="/state/djibouti">Djibouti</a></li></ul></section>
